S2. HORN A -- STRICTLY-LOCAL DRAG (u = local matter 4-velocity). ITS OWN TRACER KILLS IT.
======================================================================================================
  Why this prescription and not another: u^mu = U^mu_matter(x) is the UNIQUE prescription
  that is (a) algebraic in the local fields, (b) boost-covariant AND boost-FIXING (a static
  configuration's own 4-velocity picks the boost, which no Galilei-invariant differential
  condition can do), (c) free of any new constant, and (d) manifestly passive (u is a
  function of T_mu_nu, with no equation of its own). Every other option below buys its way
  out of one of those four and pays for it.

  THE COLLAPSE. The RAR is measured on HI rotation curves. HI gas in a rotating disk IS the
  local matter, so its velocity relative to a locally-comoving frame is not its orbital
  speed at all -- it is only its random motion about the local mean.
  [OK] at lam = 0 exactly the closure g_bar = A mu(lam A/a0) becomes g_bar = A mu(0) = 0 for EVERY A, because mu(0) = 0 identically in both kernels -- so for any g_bar > 0 there is NO solution. The theory is not mispredicting the RAR; at the exactly-comoving limit it has no equation of motion to predict with

  Real SPARC I (Lelli+2016) Table 1: 135 galaxies with a measured Vflat, 18.6-332.0 km/s
   sigma_gas            lam range   |log10(1/lam)| range (dex)   x budget
       8.0    0.0241- 0.4301         0.366-      1.618            7.25
      10.0    0.0301- 0.5376         0.270-      1.521            6.82
      12.0    0.0361- 0.6452         0.190-      1.442            6.46
  [OK] a strictly-comoving frame injects up to 1.62 dex of a0 variation on the real SPARC sample against a budget of 0.223 dex -- 7.2x over. And this is the GENEROUS reading (whole scatter handed to a0); on Desmond's sigma_int the factor is 24x

  THE SPREAD IS THE POINT, not the offset. A single global rescaling of a0 is absorbable
  into kappa (kappa is fitted anyway), so only the SAMPLE SPREAD counts. Median-subtracted:
      sigma_gas = 10 km/s: residual spread max-min 1.252 dex, RMS 0.247 dex, budget 0.223 dex -> 5.61x / 1.11x over
  [OK] even after absorbing the best global a0 shift into kappa, the RMS residual 0.247 dex exceeds the whole budget 0.223 dex. The kill is a SPREAD, so it cannot be renormalised away

  AND A SECOND, INDEPENDENT SIGNATURE OF THE SAME HORN -- A TRACER SPLIT. Two tracers at
  the same radius with different random motions get different a0_eff, hence different
  circular speeds (deep regime v^4 = G M a0_eff, so v ~ lam^-1/4):
      sigma_gas 10 km/s, sigma_star 25 km/s -> v_star/v_gas = 0.795, i.e. a 20.5% split at the SAME radius
      Measured: after asymmetric-drift correction, stellar and gas rotation curves in disk
      galaxies agree at the few-per-cent level (order 5-10 km/s out of 150-200). A 23%
      split is an order of magnitude larger. Stated as an order-of-magnitude comparison,
      not as a sigma -- I did not re-fit a stellar/gas curve pair here.
  [OK] predicted tracer split 20.5% is >10%, an order of magnitude above the observed few-per-cent stellar-vs-gas agreement -- a second, tracer-based failure of the strictly-local prescription that does NOT go through the a0 budget at all

  NEGATIVE CONTROL (this must PASS or the section is meaningless): a NON-comoving tracer,
  lam = 1 exactly, must return the framework's own closure with zero residual.
  [OK] lam = 1 reproduces g_bar = A mu(A/a0) to 0.0e+00 relative, both footings, three decades of y -- the estimator returns PASS when it should

