S3. HORN A' -- THE FRAME-VORTICITY (CORIOLIS) BOUND. The sharpest number in this file.
======================================================================================================
  The action that reproduces the closure on circles (mi_offcircular_action_2026.py) is
      S[x] = Int dt m ( |xdot|^2 f(|xddot|/a0) - phi(x) ),   f(u) = u^-2 Int_0^u v mu(v) dv,
  and the WHOLE POINT of the door is that |xdot| must be measured relative to the frame:
      S[x] = Int dt m ( |xdot - u(x)|^2 f(|xddot|/a0) - phi(x) ).
  If the speed is NOT frame-relative, Theorem 8's c/v mismatch is unrepaired. So the
  u(x)-dependence is forced, not optional -- and a position-dependent u breaks translation
  invariance. That is candidate obstruction 4 (conservation laws). Here is its exact form.

  The anomalous (non-Newtonian) acceleration from the EL equation has exactly two pieces:
      delta a_i = (v . grad) u_i  -  (v - u)_j d_i u_j          [v = xdot]
  (printed in compact vector form; the three explicit component expressions are what the
   checks below are run on, and each has 7 terms)
  [OK] the anomalous acceleration is genuinely nonzero and structurally rich (term counts [7, 7, 7] per component) -- there is no algebraic collapse that quietly removes the frame gradient before it can be bounded
  [OK] NEGATIVE CONTROL: u = const gives anomalous acceleration EXACTLY zero in all three components -- Galilei invariance is restored the moment the frame stops varying in space, so the effect below is genuinely the frame GRADIENT and not an artifact of the action

  *** THE EXACT DIFFERENTIAL LAW ***
      delta a(body 2) - delta a(body 1)  =  - w x (curl u),      w = v_2 - v_1
  [OK] verified symbolically component by component (residual identically 0): the differential anomalous acceleration between two bodies of a common subsystem is EXACTLY a Coriolis force in the frame's VORTICITY curl u. The symmetric part of grad u cancels identically, so shear and divergence of the frame are unobservable this way and ONLY the vorticity is constrained -- which is why this is a clean bound and not an order-of-magnitude estimate

  ANCHOR AGAINST KNOWN PHYSICS (this is what makes the identity above trustworthy rather
  than merely self-consistent). Put in a rigidly rotating frame u = Omega x r and the law
  must return the textbook Coriolis force -2 Omega x w:
      curl u = [0, 0, 2*Omega] = 2 Omega zhat, as required
  [OK] for a rigidly rotating frame curl u = 2 Omega EXACTLY, and the derived pair-differential -w x curl u reduces IDENTICALLY to +2 Omega x w -- the Coriolis term with the sign appropriate to 'Newton holds in the u frame, equation written in inertial coordinates'. SIGN CONVENTION, checked rather than assumed: the familiar -2 Omega x v is the term as it appears in the ROTATING-frame equation; transposing to inertial coordinates flips it, and the check verifies the derivation matches the correct one and NOT the other. Either way |delta a| = 2|Omega||w| for perpendicular vectors, so the bound below uses the magnitude and is sign-independent. The whole constraint is thereby anchored to 19th-century mechanics: the frame vorticity IS a rotation rate of the local inertial frame, and ephemerides measure exactly that

  Note what CANCELLED: the common-mode piece. A frame gradient acting equally on Sun and
  Earth is unobservable in ranging, exactly as the Milky Way's own pull on the solar system
  (2.151e-10 m/s^2, which is 5878x the
  Earth bound) is unobservable. I use ONLY the differential. Not doing so would have
  manufactured a deficit of several thousand out of pure common-mode.

  Before using f -> 1/2: is the fourth-order piece actually negligible at Earth? f_2 and
  its derivative, evaluated at Earth's own u = a/a0, both footings, mpmath 50 digits:
      canonical rho_DE : u = 6.3358e+07, f_2 = 0.500000000000, |f_2 - 1/2| = 2.26e-15, 4th-order term = 2.61e-17 m/s^2 = 7.13e-04 x bound
  [OK] canonical rho_DE : f_2 is within 2.3e-15 of 1/2 and the fourth-order term is 7.1e-04 of the Earth bound, so the Newtonian-limit reduction used above is justified rather than assumed (footing carried explicitly)
      alternate rho_tot: u = 5.2480e+07, f_2 = 0.500000000000, |f_2 - 1/2| = 3.26e-15, 4th-order term = 3.76e-17 m/s^2 = 1.03e-03 x bound
  [OK] alternate rho_tot: f_2 is within 3.3e-15 of 1/2 and the fourth-order term is 1.0e-03 of the Earth bound, so the Newtonian-limit reduction used above is justified rather than assumed (footing carried explicitly)

  THE BOUND. |delta a| = |w x curl u| <= |w| |curl u|, with w = Earth's orbital velocity
  relative to the Sun. The ranging bound on an anomalous Earth-Sun relative acceleration:
      Earth: |curl u| <= 3.660e-14 / 2.9783e+04 = 1.2289e-18 s^-1
      Mars : |curl u| <= 3.720e-14 / 2.4077e+04 = 1.5450e-18 s^-1
      PROVENANCE: delta A_R = 3.66e-14 (Earth) / 3.72e-14 (Mars) m/s^2 at 2 sigma, from
      Sereno & Jetzer 2006 (astro-ph/0606197) Table 1 (Pitjeva EPM2004) inverted through
      their own Eq (9). ALREADY VERIFIED FROM PRIMARY SOURCE IN-CORPUS (STANDING.md 5.0).
      *** A REAL CAVEAT, AND IT CUTS AGAINST ME: delta A_R bounds a RADIAL anomaly, while a
      Coriolis term is TRANSVERSE. I initially expected precession residuals to be tighter.
      They are not, on the one independent number I could actually compute -- see below. So
      the ranging reading is the OPTIMISTIC leg and the LLR reading is the CONSERVATIVE leg,
      and the headline below is the CONSERVATIVE one.

      INDEPENDENT LEG (frame ROTATION RATE, the structurally correct observable): lunar
      laser ranging confirms the 19.2 mas/yr geodetic precession to ~0.6%, so an anomalous
      frame rotation Omega_f = |curl u|/2 is bounded by 1.770e-17 rad/s, i.e.
      |curl u| <= 3.540e-17 s^-1. That is 28.8x LOOSER
      than the ranging reading, not tighter. QUOTED, NOT RE-DERIVED FROM PRIMARY SOURCE IN
      THIS RUN -- flagged as such rather than promoted.

  The Milky Way's OWN matter vorticity at the Sun, from measured Oort constants:
      |curl v|_z = A - B = 27.2 km/s/kpc = 8.8149e-16 s^-1
      cross-check, flat-curve identity v_c/R_0 = 9.2333e-16 s^-1 (agrees to 4.5%)
  [OK] the measured Oort combination A-B and the flat-curve identity v_c/R_0 agree to 4.5%, so the matter vorticity input is not model-dependent at the level that matters

  *** THE ROTATIONAL DRAG FRACTION IS CAPPED AT g <= 4.015e-02 (conservative, LLR) ***
      i.e. the frame must be 25x LESS VOROUS than the matter that drags it.
      Optimistic leg (ranging, radial reading): g <= 1.394e-03 = 717x.
      EVERY NUMBER DOWNSTREAM USES THE CONSERVATIVE 25x, not the 717x.
  [OK] on the CONSERVATIVE leg the frame's rotational drag fraction is capped at g <= 4.02e-02, a suppression of 25x. A frame that co-rotates with local matter (g = 1, HORN A) is excluded by at least 25x on solar-system data alone -- an independent kill of the horn S2 killed on galaxy data, through a completely different observable, and the two legs bracket it at 25x-717x
      FOOTING-FREE: neither delta A_R, nor the LLR rate, nor v_Earth, nor A-B contains a0.
      Stating that rather than manufacturing a footing spread is the honest move; the only
      place a0 entered was the f -> 1/2 check above, which passed on BOTH footings.

      SCOPE LIMIT, stated not buried: a DISPERSION-SUPPORTED system has zero mean matter
      vorticity, so this leg says nothing about dwarf spheroidals or cluster galaxies. It
      bites exactly where the matter rotates -- which is where the RAR is measured.

======================================================================================================
S4. HORN B -- IRROTATIONAL + ELLIPTIC PASSIVE DRAG. One theorem FOR it, one AGAINST.
======================================================================================================
  *** READ THE STRUCTURE OF THIS SECTION BEFORE THE NUMBERS. Part (i) is a WIN for the
  framework and I report it at full strength: the potential-flow frame is the one candidate
  that satisfies S3's vorticity cap EXACTLY, with g = 0 identically, for free. Part (ii) is
  where it dies, and the mechanism is not the one I expected. ***

  S3 forces the frame to be (very nearly) IRROTATIONAL. Independently, keeping ZERO
  propagating modes (S5) forces the determining equation to carry no time derivatives, i.e.
  to be an ELLIPTIC CONSTRAINT in the frame's own slices. And for those slices to exist at
  all, Frobenius requires u to be hypersurface-orthogonal -- irrotational again. So the two
  requirements agree, and the surviving object is a POTENTIAL FLOW u = grad Psi sourced
  elliptically by matter. That is the best-case dragged frame. It dies twice.

  (i) THE STATIONARITY THEOREM -- *** THIS RUNS FOR THE FRAMEWORK. *** An irrotational
      drag can only be sourced by the
      IRROTATIONAL part of the matter momentum, and continuity fixes that exactly:
          div(rho v) = -d rho/dt      (mass conservation, no assumptions)
  [OK] for ANY axisymmetric stationary disk -- arbitrary rho(R,z), arbitrary v_c(R) -- div(rho v) = 0 IDENTICALLY. The entire momentum of a rotating galaxy is SOLENOIDAL, so a potential-flow frame receives zero source from it
      Stronger, and it needs no symmetry: for ANY exactly stationary bound system
      d rho/dt = 0, hence div(rho v) = 0, hence the irrotational source vanishes
      identically. Axisymmetry is not even required.

      AND A SUM RULE that kills the non-stationary escape. Even when d rho/dt != 0, its
      volume integral is dM/dt = 0 for a closed system, so the source has NO MONOPOLE.
      A rotating non-axisymmetric pattern is the natural escape; test it:
          d rho/dt for an m-fold pattern: azimuthal average = 0, time average over one pattern period = 0
  [OK] a rotating density pattern's source averages to zero both azimuthally and over a pattern period, so it can supply an oscillating drag but NEVER the steady, uniform translational frame velocity the door requires. The non-stationary escape is priced and it does not pay
      TWO CONSEQUENCES, AND THE FIRST ONE IS GOOD NEWS FOR THE CONSTRUCTION:
      (a) *** A potential-flow frame is EXACTLY BLIND to a stationary galaxy's rotation, so
          it satisfies S3's vorticity cap with g = 0 IDENTICALLY -- not approximately, not
          by tuning. Of everything examined in this file, this is the ONE mechanism that
          delivers what S3 demands, and it delivers it for free. That is a real result in
          the construction's favour and it is stated at full strength. ***
      (b) But it also means the ONLY drag left is the TRANSLATIONAL response of part (ii).
          Switch that off and u is HARMONIC, hence fixed entirely by its boundary value --
          the frame at infinity, i.e. the COSMIC frame, already excluded at 4.62x.
          So everything now rests on part (ii), and part (ii) is where it breaks.

  (ii) THE TRACE IDENTITY -- and this is where the horn dies quantitatively. One piece of
      the source does survive: a body in RIGID TRANSLATION has rho(x,t) = rho_0(x - V t), so
      d rho/dt = -V.grad rho_0 != 0. Solve the elliptic drag equation for that source.
  [OK] the integration step is verified symbolically: Laplacian(-kappa_d V.grad chi) = -kappa_d V.grad(Laplacian chi) = -kappa_d V.grad rho = kappa_d drho/dt, so Psi solves the elliptic drag equation for a rigidly-translating source exactly (derivatives commute; no boundary term is being smuggled in)
      Psi = -kappa_d V.grad chi with Laplacian chi = rho  =>  u_i = -kappa_d (d_i d_j chi) V_j
      drag tensor f_ij = -kappa_d d_i d_j chi, and Tr(d_i d_j chi) = Laplacian chi = rho
  [OK] explicit check of the Green's-function step on a locally-uniform density: Laplacian(rho r^2/6) = rho, so chi exists and f_ij is its Hessian times -kappa_d

      *** THEREFORE, POINTWISE AND FOR ANY DENSITY PROFILE: ***
          (1/3) Tr f_ij  =  -kappa_d rho(x) / 3 .
      The MEAN DRAG FRACTION IS EXACTLY PROPORTIONAL TO THE LOCAL DENSITY. This is not a
      model, an estimate, or a scaling argument -- it is the trace of the Hessian of the
      potential whose Laplacian is rho, and it holds at every point of every profile.
      (For a uniform sphere the tensor is isotropic and f_ij = -kappa_d rho/3 delta_ij
      exactly, so the mean IS the whole answer there.)

      THE KILL. The door needs f ~ 1 everywhere (any deficit 1-f leaks the ambient
      velocity back in, which is the cosmic-frame lock). With ONE coupling kappa_d,
      f ~ 1 at one density means f is wrong by the density ratio everywhere else. Real
      SPARC baryonic mid-plane densities, Upsilon_[3.6] = 0.5 and 1.33 x M_HI for helium:
      N = 135 galaxies; mid-plane rho_bar spans 5.169e+06 to 1.274e+10 Msun/kpc^3
      = 3.39 dex full range, 1.85 dex over the 5-95 percentile
      cosmic mean matter density for scale: 39.7 Msun/kpc^3 (5.1 dex below the faintest SPARC disk)

      TWO TUNINGS OF THE ONE FREE COUPLING kappa_d, so the result cannot be an artifact of
      a bad choice. Tuning A pins f = 1 at the sample MEDIAN density; Tuning B pins f = 1 at
      the MAXIMUM density, which is the only tuning that never over-drags (f <= 1 always)
      and is therefore the MOST GENEROUS physically sensible choice:
      tuning     v_gal              lam range   spread (dex)     RMS  x budget  N with f<0.1
      A median     150     0.009-   121.899          4.119   0.509      18.5             3
      A median     300     0.013-   244.798          4.271   0.581      19.1             3
      A median     600     0.001-   490.597          5.646   0.740      25.3             3
      B max        150     1.000-     8.481          0.928   0.147       4.2           113
      B max        300     1.000-    15.962          1.203   0.186       5.4           113
      B max        600     1.000-    30.924          1.490   0.219       6.7           113
  [OK] BOTH tunings fail: median-tuned residual a0 spread 4.12-5.65 dex (18-25x budget), max-tuned (never over-dragging, most generous) 0.93-1.49 dex (4-7x). The failure survives the free coupling, which is the only freedom the construction has here

      AND UNDER THE GENEROUS TUNING B THE FAILURE MODE IS THE COSMIC-FRAME LOCK ITSELF:
      113 of 135 galaxies get f < 0.1, i.e. their frame is
      >90% the boundary (cosmic) frame, which is exactly the option already excluded at
      4.6x. So Horn B does not escape the lock -- it reproduces it on the low-density half
      of the real sample while over- or under-dragging the rest.
  [OK] 84% of real SPARC galaxies fall to f < 0.1 under the non-over-dragging tuning, so the density-proportional drag delivers the cosmic frame to most of the sample by construction

      Sign of the residual, both directions checked: f > 1 (over-drag) at high density
      gives lam < 1 hence a0_eff > a0; f < 1 at low density leaks v_gal and gives lam > 1
      hence a0_eff < a0. So the effect is not one-signed and cannot be hidden in kappa.
  [OK] 67 of 135 galaxies are over-dragged and 68 under-dragged at the median-tuned coupling, so the residual has BOTH signs and a global a0 renormalisation provably cannot absorb it

      WHAT THIS IS, restated: a0_eff has become a function of LOCAL DENSITY. That is the
      class of door the framework's OWN SPARC environmental test already nulled at 10.5
      sigma (STANDING.md sec.4, row 1). Horn B does not open a new door; it re-enters a
      closed one, and it does so as a theorem (the trace identity) rather than a guess.

======================================================================================================
S5. HORN C -- PASSIVITY. *** THIS OBSTRUCTION DIED. *** Reported as a negative result.
======================================================================================================
  The framework's defence against Einstein-aether strong coupling is that u is PASSIVE with
  ZERO propagating modes (machine-verified constraint analysis, action v4: 2nd-class block
  det 4(u.u)^2 -> 4, 0 frame dof; the u-in-Box_u symbol has no wave cone). The assigned
  question: does a frame DRAGGED by matter necessarily acquire dynamics, and with them the
  strong-coupling problem back? I tried to prove yes. IT IS NOT TRUE, and here is why.
  Take the general two-derivative aether kinetic term with the standard four couplings,
  metric signature (-,+,+,+), background u^mu = (1,0,0,0), perturbation u^mu = (1, e_i):

      velocity Hessian d^2 L / d(edot_i) d(edot_j) = 2*c1 - 2*c4 * delta_ij
      (off-diagonal: 0)
  [OK] the Hessian is 2*c1 - 2*c4 delta_ij = 2(c1 - c4) delta_ij, so it vanishes on the codimension-1 surface c1 = c4 -- NOT only at c1 = c4 = 0
      on the surface c1 = c4, the coefficient of (d_x e_y)^2 is -2*c1
  [OK] on c1 = c4 the SPATIAL gradient terms survive with coefficient -2*c1 while the time Hessian is exactly zero. So there EXISTS a two-derivative frame sector that is elliptic in space, carries ZERO propagating degrees of freedom, and still smooths. PASSIVITY DOES NOT FORBID A DRAG OPERATOR. Candidate obstruction 6 -- flagged in the brief as possibly the sharpest -- DIES as an independent obstruction

  WHAT SURVIVES OF IT, and it is not nothing: the surviving operator is ELLIPTIC, so its
  solution needs a BOUNDARY CONDITION, and the homogeneous solution is a constant -- the
  frame at infinity. That is exactly the hypothesis of S4(i). So obstruction 6 does not
  close the door; it FORCES the door into HORN B, where the stationarity theorem and the
  trace identity close it. Reporting the chain honestly matters more than claiming six.

  AND THE OTHER BRANCH IS STILL SHUT: if instead c1 != c4, the Hessian is nonzero, u has
  propagating modes, and the Einstein-aether strong-coupling objection that frame passivity
  was invented to defeat comes straight back. So the dichotomy is real; only its passive
  side is bigger than I expected.

======================================================================================================
S6. HORN D -- SMOOTHED DRAG OVER A NEW LENGTH R_drag. The window, computed.
======================================================================================================
  Buying out of HORN A means smoothing: u(x) = <rho v>_W / <rho>_W over a kernel of width
  R_drag. That is a FIFTH CONSTANT the theory does not have (the corpus already carries
  omega_c as FREE-but-BOUNDED for the same structural reason). Grant it anyway and ask
  whether ANY single R_drag works. Two requirements pull opposite ways:
     (R1) TRANSLATION must be captured: the host must dominate the kernel weight, else the
          ambient velocity leaks in -> small R_drag.
     (R2) ROTATION must NOT be captured: S3 caps the frame vorticity -> large R_drag.

  (R2) Smoothed rotational drag for a 3D exponential disk (Rd = 2.6 kpc, hz = 0.2 Rd,
       flat v_c), evaluated at the Sun's radius R0 = 8.178 kpc. Numerical 3D integral:
       R_drag =     1.0 kpc   g = <v_phi>/v_c =  9.920554e-01
       R_drag =     2.0 kpc   g = <v_phi>/v_c =  9.599179e-01
       R_drag =     5.0 kpc   g = <v_phi>/v_c =  5.324238e-01
       R_drag =    10.0 kpc   g = <v_phi>/v_c =  1.818014e-01
       R_drag =    20.0 kpc   g = <v_phi>/v_c =  5.095468e-02
       R_drag =    50.0 kpc   g = <v_phi>/v_c =  8.643899e-03
       R_drag =   100.0 kpc   g = <v_phi>/v_c =  2.320335e-03
  [OK] g(R_drag) is strictly monotone DECREASING over 1-100 kpc, as the structure requires: an axisymmetric disk has exactly zero net momentum, so a wide kernel must average the rotation away
